---- RATIONALE ----

>> A3: Why does Pintos implement strtok_r() but not strtok()?
strtok() uses a hidden global variable to track its position between function calls.
This is not thread safe, because if two threads tokenize strings at the same time,
they'd corrupt each other. 

strtok_r takes a save_ptr instead, making it safe to use in a multi-threaded kernel like Pintos
where multiple threads may be tokenizing strings at the same time.


>> A4: In Pintos, the kernel separates commands into a executable name
>> and arguments.  In Unix-like systems, the shell does this
>> separation.  Identify at least two advantages of the Unix approach.

-> Users can use different shells with different syntaxes, parsing, etc
   without making changes to the kernel (i.e: more flexible/modular and 
   reduces kernel crashes/bugs)

-> The kernel only needs to exec a prog with an already parsed executable
   so it does not need to understand how to parse, tokenize, etc
   other than running the file which makes the kernel more modular

